WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.659
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Seth Moskowitz, who's a journalist writing

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:08.559
about American politics and technology and
an editor at Persuasion Magazine.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.929
He's also author of the article The Reactionary
Trap, which I've talked about before with

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:14.929
my audience.

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:16.410
Seth, really great having you on today.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.260
Speaker 3: Thank you very much for having
me.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.260
I'm excited to be here.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.330
Speaker 1: So, I mean, just just to start
with, let's talk about I have made the case

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.920
to my audience that much of what is currently
associated with the American right wing is

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.790
not really conservative by any definition
of the term, but is really a sort of neo reactionary

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:36.890
movement.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:40.050
Can you talk a little bit about when we say
reactionary?

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.670
What what are the characteristics of a reactionary
political movement or policy or positions?

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.110
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.450
Well, fundamentally, what a reactionary is.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.489
Somebody who wants to go back to the way things
were before, politically or socially.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:55.739
Essentially, they want to roll back the clock.

00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:01.360
So we're a progressive might want to see some
form of change to push things into the future.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.240
Conservative might be a little bit more focused
on tradition and skeptical of change, but

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.650
maybe more open to it a reactionary somebody
who wants to roll back the clock to the way

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.600
things were before.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.890
And so in my article, I lay out two things
that I think kind of are hallmarks of a reactionary.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:23.110
The first is that they lose sight of any sense
of proportionality, something that they see

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.110
as a threat.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.130
They are not able to kind of judge if it's
a five alarm fire or if it's something that's

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.850
kind of a minor threat that they should be
minor league concerns about.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.400
And the second is that their politics becomes
one of opposition.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.590
They lose sight of their their principles
and their values and the things that they

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.970
want to see in the world, and they become
focused only on opposing things that they

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:45.970
dislike.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.570
Speaker 1: Now, in hearing that it really
sounds like Trumpism, right?

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:55.330
I mean, like when I hear you say that, I think,
OK, so a movement of opposition.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.150
Here's something Joe Biden wants to do.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.159
Nope, we're not going to.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:01:59.540
We don't want him to do that.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.729
Here's something Barack Obama did.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:07.040
Now here's why we're going to undo it without
really bringing policy proposals to the table.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.440
And also the fear piece, which is critical.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.880
Race theory is going to be a disaster with
no proportionality in terms of is it actually

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:22.730
something that's being taught trans bathroom
issues or trans women in sports with no sense

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.790
of the scope of these issues?

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.900
Am I right in applying it in this way?

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.340
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think so.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.849
And I think the important thing that I've
noticed is that really what it feels like

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:37.400
to me is our dividing line in politics seems
to be this culture war.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.680
And I think what happens with reactionaries
is they kind of glom onto the politics of

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.380
the day and the thing that's making people
most furious and most angry.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.060
And today, that's the culture, war and social
issues.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.430
So when I think about who is a reactionary
in contemporary politics, I don't think about

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.440
what their positions are on tax policy or
health care as much.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.879
Because really, it seems like today in politics,
you can kind of have opinions on those issues

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:03.290
and be on the left or right.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.630
What really matters and what really feels
like a dividing line in politics today is

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.069
cultural issues and social issues.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:14.850
So the reactionaries, that is your focus,
like you said on critical race theory and

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.980
school on the current.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.040
Fear about gender being taught in elementary
schools.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.760
Black Lives Matter, all these cultural social
issues that seem to have taken over our politics

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.760
today.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.420
I think those are white reactionaries or glomming
on to and are really talking and saying, we

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.590
see these changes that are happening and we
want to go back to the world before those

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.920
changes were happening now.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.390
Speaker 1: In the article, the reactionary
trap, which we will link to you, also point

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:50.950
out the left is not immune from this, and
also that social media has a role to play

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:56.849
in kind of creating these fear based, reactionary,
almost like feedback loop.

00:03:56.849 --> 00:03:58.489
So can you talk about that a little bit?

00:03:58.489 --> 00:03:59.659
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:04.440
So in the article, I talk about a few people
who were on the left and kind of jumped the

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.879
shark and became reactionaries like I was
speaking about on these cultural and social

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:10.879
issues.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.690
And the point that I wanted to make is that
I think that people on the left need to be

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.669
aware that it's possible for them to become
reactionary, even if they think, Look, I'm

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:22.090
left on health care and left on gun issues.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.089
I'm on the left, so I can't be reactionary.

00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:29.270
They need to be aware that because the dividing
line in politics today seems to be these social

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:36.331
issues, if you become a reactionary on somehow
every single one of these social issues, it's

00:04:36.331 --> 00:04:40.610
possible that maybe you're no longer a member
of the left, but you've become sort of a reactionary

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:47.449
and that you need to be aware of this, that
maybe you can kind of avoid a politics that

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:52.319
only relies on opposition and is only based
on fear.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:55.770
And you just start to have a positive vision
for maybe what you want your politics to look

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.869
like and you want the country to look like.

00:04:57.869 --> 00:05:02.129
And so essentially, the article talked about
a few of these people who are on the left

00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:03.499
and sort of sublimated.

00:05:03.499 --> 00:05:06.699
They skipped the middle phase and became reactionaries
on the right.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:11.189
And and I think a big part of what happened
to them is that they got involved on Twitter.

00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:15.189
They started to get a feedback loop of people
who were rewarding them for criticizing the

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:16.189
left on social issues.

00:05:16.189 --> 00:05:20.550
They both have an audience of those type of
people, and then they were unable to criticize

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:27.449
the left on any issues, and they sort of became
reactionaries out without any, any real thought

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:31.319
about what was happening to them just by following
their instincts and following their social

00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:32.319
media following.

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:36.639
Speaker 1: Yeah, I can kind of understand
how it can happen because, you know, over

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:42.409
the last many years, I've waded into topics
like, for example, when the term regressive

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:44.279
left was a big thing.

00:05:44.279 --> 00:05:47.499
I was glad to say, Hey, you know what?

00:05:47.499 --> 00:05:51.770
There is this thing of people who operate
under the label of being left, but they're

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.680
really quite authoritarian.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.330
And that's not a leftism that I recognize,
right?

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.369
For example, which to me is relatively uncontroversial.

00:05:58.369 --> 00:06:01.419
Like, these things exist in any political
movement.

00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:07.419
And there was a reaction, a very loud reaction
from a slice of my audience focused exclusively

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:12.289
on that, which if I had just latched on to
that, it probably would have pulled me into

00:06:12.289 --> 00:06:14.240
a completely different audience.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.539
Maybe it would have been lucrative.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:17.139
Maybe it would have, but would have allowed
growth.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.439
I was only interested in going as far as I
believe the facts suggested on that, but it

00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:22.520
seems like you can.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:26.599
You will very quickly start hearing from a
slice of your audience and they will pull

00:06:26.599 --> 00:06:27.929
you towards those views.

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:31.749
Speaker 3: I think that's 100 percent right
and it's oftentimes going to be the loudest

00:06:31.749 --> 00:06:33.860
and most active part of your audience.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.879
And so you're going to see all these people
reacting.

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:40.139
The thing that you said that was maybe the
most exciting and you're going to say, Oh,

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.059
wow, maybe this is what's getting my audience
excited as you give them more of this content.

00:06:44.059 --> 00:06:45.610
It's it's called audience capture.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:50.580
And I think it's something that the people
who checks on Twitter and a lot of these people

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.789
who are maybe more reactionary on their social
views get a lot of a response from those people

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:58.330
on Twitter and they get really pulled into
that world.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.379
And I don't think it's I don't think it's
only something that happened with the right.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:03.199
I think it can happen on the left, too.

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:08.240
And I think it just happens to be that it's
the most radical sides of either base that

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.780
really, really is the most active on Twitter
and on social media.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:17.419
And it's really pulling people to the most
to the most radical parts of their party or

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:18.830
their social movement.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:25.330
I think it's honestly particularly noticeable
on the right on Twitter only because it's

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:32.069
it seems like it brings out the most vile
part of people, and it really gets them excited

00:07:32.069 --> 00:07:34.680
to see it on Twitter.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.860
When you're just when you're scrolling through
it and it gives you an emotional reaction

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:45.289
and you end and pulls out a side of you that
wants to respond and wants to get engaged.

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:48.580
And I think it's important to take a step
back and say, Well, this isn't how the world

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:49.580
actually is.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:53.629
This is just a certain portion of Twitter
that's really this reactionary in this grotesque.

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:55.309
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:07:55.309 --> 00:08:00.240
And I find that I will often get questions
like as an example there.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.860
I don't know if you remember this, but shortly
after Trump was elected in 2016, we saw the

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.899
rise of the Women's March.

00:08:06.899 --> 00:08:11.539
And then there was this controversy where
some of the Women's March leaders felt that

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:18.229
Jewish women were sort of like not intersectional
enough or not discriminated against enough.

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:25.229
To qualify for like board or leadership membership,
I called that out wholeheartedly and talked

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:31.189
about, you know, the dismissal of anti-Semitism
and Jewish disgrace, anti-Jewish discrimination.

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:34.800
But I also recognize like this is a pretty
small slice of the left.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.409
It was sort of like an attempt to keep that
proportionality in check, as you mentioned.

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:41.099
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think that's really important.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:45.540
I think there was a study that was done on
the Hidden Tribe study, and it said like seven

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.830
percent of of users on Twitter are the most
are progressive activists.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:55.300
About seven percent are conservative reactionaries
or something equivalent on the right.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:09:00.509
Whether they create the vast majority of the
content on Twitter, and I think that's because

00:09:00.509 --> 00:09:04.310
one they're probably the most excited about
their political projects, the most active

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:09.360
and two, I think that they make it so that
the rest of the users on Twitter are fearful

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.360
of of saying anything or fearful of using
the platform because they might get attacked

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.380
by one or either side of those of the political
spectrum, right?

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.900
And so I think, you
Speaker 1: know what I was, I was going to

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:27.250
say, what are the if we have an interest in
having some tools with which we can sort of

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:31.300
like pump the brakes when we start getting
sucked into some of these things?

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.889
You sort of alluded to some of these things,
which is one is asking how big of a problem

00:09:34.889 --> 00:09:35.889
is this really?

00:09:35.889 --> 00:09:40.269
What are some of the other questions that
one can kind of ask themselves as they start

00:09:40.269 --> 00:09:42.509
getting pulled into some of these reactionary
loops?

00:09:42.509 --> 00:09:46.990
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think the first and foremost
thing is to make sure that you have principles

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:52.089
and values that you're following, rather than
just reacting and seeing something and saying,

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:57.160
Oh, that scares me or I don't like that, I'm
going to go on the other side of that issue.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:01.990
I think it's really important for people to
think through what are my values, what is

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:07.250
my political project and what is the positive
vision that I have for the world.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:11.759
The second is being willing to consider the
possibility that you're wrong.

00:10:11.759 --> 00:10:16.240
I think it's really important for people to
understand that they're not always right.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.860
And one of the amazing things about Twitter
is that we can outsource asking questions

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:22.170
we can outsource.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:26.339
We only have one brain, and when there's millions
of brains on Twitters on Twitter, I think

00:10:26.339 --> 00:10:28.040
it's useful to think, OK, maybe I'm wrong.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:33.930
I need to really consider what other people
are pushing back on me for or the ideas that

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.899
they're bringing to the table.

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:41.470
And then the last one, I think, is to avoid
us versus them thinking it's really easy to

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:45.930
fall into the trap where you say, This is
my side, I'm going to agree with everything

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:50.120
on this side and everyone who is on the other
side of this political issue or on the other

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:55.850
side of the political divide has bad motives
or is wrong on every single issue.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:11:00.819
And so I think if you do those three things,
one, make sure you have a positive vision

00:11:00.819 --> 00:11:05.670
to don't, don't fall into us versus them thinking.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:10.980
And then three, be able to consider the possibility
that you're wrong, then you'll avoid this,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.630
this reactionary thinking that a lot of people
have fallen into.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:18.721
Speaker 1: What do you suspect happened with
someone like, for example, Dave Rubin, who

00:11:18.721 --> 00:11:20.200
you write about in the article?

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:24.019
You know, I used to be quite friendly with
Dave, and we would text and chat on the phone,

00:11:24.019 --> 00:11:29.220
sometimes years and years ago and more recently,
you know, he's attacked me publicly.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:34.980
He's kind of sent me text privately and like
my view has been, I don't know if he was like

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:39.090
38 or 40 at the time that this political reawakening
kind of happened.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:44.100
But I've not really met people in my life
whose politics so dramatically changes at

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:48.250
that age unless they were almost like not
even engaged at all.

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:50.980
And they didn't even really have, like a formative
politics.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.569
So it's hard for me to imagine that his views
really changed.

00:11:53.569 --> 00:11:57.899
So it's easy to say he was captured by the
audience and he was captured by, you know,

00:11:57.899 --> 00:12:00.200
getting to know Trump and this type of thing.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.149
But I just don't really know what to make
of it like.

00:12:03.149 --> 00:12:04.999
What's your sense of that particular example?

00:12:04.999 --> 00:12:09.110
Speaker 3: Yeah, it's hard to get into into
people's heads, but generally what I think,

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:14.009
I think happens to a lot of these people is
that they go against.

00:12:14.009 --> 00:12:19.009
Well, one I think it's important to note that
the mid 2010s, the early 2010s and when Trump

00:12:19.009 --> 00:12:22.949
came along, there was a lot of social change
in terms of Black Lives Matter.

00:12:22.949 --> 00:12:26.920
And there was a lot of this cultural change
that made people fearful.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:31.230
So I think that brought out a certain type
of person who maybe was progressive on other

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:35.519
issues, but then they realized that they maybe
they're more reactionary on these more cultural

00:12:35.519 --> 00:12:36.740
issues.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:42.000
The second thing I think that happens a lot
is that maybe someone who is on the left goes

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:48.899
against one of the progressive dogmas or one
of the progressive cataclysms on these progressive

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:49.899
issues.

00:12:49.899 --> 00:12:53.470
And then they get really dogpile by people
on the left and they think, Whoa, whoa, what

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:54.470
has happened?

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:55.760
And I thought these were my allies.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.009
I thought these were my friends.

00:12:58.009 --> 00:13:02.959
And then they kind of, rather than think,
having a somewhat rational reaction and thinking,

00:13:02.959 --> 00:13:04.330
OK, either one.

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:07.029
Maybe I got this issue wrong or two.

00:13:07.029 --> 00:13:11.410
Maybe the rest of my team is being a little
bit irrational on this issue.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:16.470
They kind of jumped to the other team and
then the other team or the other side accepts

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:17.990
them because.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.839
They say, look, now we have someone from the
left on our side.

00:13:20.839 --> 00:13:23.060
And I think that it gives them more credibility.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.829
Speaker 1: And in a sense, it does.

00:13:24.829 --> 00:13:27.999
I mean, these folks are very useful to the
right, it seems to me.

00:13:27.999 --> 00:13:32.589
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think it is true because
when people, when people on the right say,

00:13:32.589 --> 00:13:36.510
Look, we even have people on the left who
are joining us on this issue, this issue,

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:37.510
this issue.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:42.689
And they once were really like, the young
Turks are a far progressive left and they

00:13:42.689 --> 00:13:47.399
can say, Oh, the left has kind of just gone
crazy on these issues.

00:13:47.399 --> 00:13:51.629
And maybe, maybe there is some time for reflection
on both sides like the left could say, really

00:13:51.629 --> 00:13:57.160
think through what do we need to be dogpile
and people who go against us on one specific

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.890
issue, is it really worth it?

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:03.220
Ostracizing somebody and kicking them out
of our club just because they go against one

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:04.499
of these?

00:14:04.499 --> 00:14:10.970
One of our progressive dogmas and the people
who are leading the left need to really reconsider.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:15.870
Look, are these the people that are really,
truly align with on all my political beliefs?

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:20.100
Or am I making just kind of a rash reaction
to being attacked online?

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.660
Speaker 1: Yeah, I think both sides are important.

